    "html": "<h1 id=\"rem0te-documentation\">Rem0te documentation</h1>\n<p>Start with the page that matches what you are doing. All of this is also inside\nthe product, searchable, under <strong>Documentation</strong> in the sidebar.</p>\n<h2 id=\"something-is-broken\">Something is broken</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\">troubleshooting.md</a></strong> — start here. Especially for\n<em>&quot;the target device is offline or does not exist&quot;</em>, which almost never means\nwhat it says.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"using-it\">Using it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/technician-guide\">technician-guide.md</a></strong> — day-to-day: find a computer,\nconnect, help someone unenrolled, review sessions.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/clients\">clients.md</a></strong> — every RustDesk client Rem0te hands out, who each\nis for, and how it learns where this server is.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/connecting\">connecting.md</a></strong> — what actually happens when you click\nConnect, the three connect paths, and why they are not equivalent.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/inventory\">inventory.md</a></strong> — the specs a managed computer reports, its\npending Windows updates, and how to read a slice of its event log.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/tactical-rmm\">tactical-rmm.md</a></strong> — connecting and querying from inside\nTactical RMM: URL Actions, agent matching, and the Collector Task.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/integration-clientst0r\">integration-clientst0r.md</a></strong> — ClientSt0r ticket\nanalysis (contract ticket-rmm-v1): staff-only findings, what a job can and\ncannot touch, peers file, mappings. Contract: <a href=\"contracts/ticket-rmm-v1/README.md\">contracts/ticket-rmm-v1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/support-requests\">support-requests.md</a></strong> — the customer support link,\nthe status page, and the technician queue.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/technicians\">technicians.md</a></strong> — MSP technicians: per-business,\ntime-limited and scheduled access, groups, approval, the business switcher.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"running-it\">Running it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/technician-access\">technician-access.md</a></strong> — technician presets (L1/L2/L3),\nlaunch policy, protected device classes, fleet-abuse limits, independent\nreview of trust changes, and what this build can and cannot enforce.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/setup\">setup.md</a></strong> — installation, configuration, ports, operations.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/updates\">updates.md</a></strong> — the three things that can be out of date\n(Rem0te, the RustDesk clients, hbbs/hbbr) and how each is updated.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/architecture\">architecture.md</a></strong> — what runs where, the data model, and\nthe RustDesk configuration chain.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/access-control\">access-control.md</a></strong> — the permission model in full.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/offboarding\">offboarding.md</a></strong> — who can reach a computer and why, and\nremoving someone&#39;s access — including what revocation cannot undo.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/sign-in\">sign-in.md</a></strong> — passwords, SSO (OIDC), passkeys, step-up, and\nrecovering from a sign-in misconfiguration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"building-on-it\">Building on it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/api-reference\">API-REFERENCE.md</a></strong> — every route, its access level and the\ncapability it needs. Generated from the controllers and checked against the\nrunning route table, so it cannot quietly fall behind.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/public-api\">PUBLIC-API.md</a></strong> — the API guide: scopes, request and\nresponse shapes, errors, worked examples.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"/docs/security-audit\">SECURITY-AUDIT.md</a></strong> — security posture and findings.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"the-one-thing-worth-knowing-up-front\">The one thing worth knowing up front</h2>\n<p>Rem0te decides <em>who may connect to what</em> and hands out the credential. RustDesk\ncarries the session. They have <strong>separate ideas of whether a computer is\nonline</strong>, and a Connect depends only on RustDesk&#39;s.</p>\n<p>When they disagree — a green dot in Rem0te and &quot;offline&quot; from RustDesk — ask\nhbbs directly rather than guessing:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo deploy/scripts/hbbs-probe.py &lt;rustdesk-id&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Most of <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\">troubleshooting.md</a> is downstream of that one\ncommand.</p>\n",
