    "slug": "support-requests",
    "title": "Support requests",
    "file": "support-requests.md",
    "html": "<h1 id=\"support-requests\">Support requests</h1>\n<p><em>Added in v0.20.</em></p>\n<p>A customer who needs help can ask for it without an account, and the request lands in a queue that the right technicians work. This is a lightweight intake and queue, not a ticketing system. There are no comments, SLAs or integrations, and requests are erased after a month.</p>\n<h2 id=\"for-the-business-the-support-link\">For the business: the support link</h2>\n<p>A Business Owner turns this on at <strong>Business Settings → Customer support link</strong>. That creates a link of the form</p>\n<pre><code>https://&lt;your server&gt;/support/&lt;16 random characters&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>which can be shared with the business&#39;s staff, put on an intranet page or a desktop shortcut. <strong>The link is the only thing that picks the business</strong>, so anyone with the link can submit a request to that business. Regenerating it invalidates the old one at once. Turning it off makes the link answer exactly as an unknown link does, so a disabled link cannot be told apart from one that never existed.</p>\n<p>Signed-in members of a business can also ask from <strong>My Computers → Request support</strong>, which can name one of their own computers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-customer-sees\">What the customer sees</h2>\n<p>The form asks for a name, contact details, a description, and optionally the RustDesk ID on their screen. After submitting, they get a <strong>status link</strong> to keep. It shows only the status in plain words (&quot;waiting for a technician&quot;, &quot;a technician is on it&quot;) and the technician&#39;s first name. It never shows a computer name, an email address or anything about any other request. The customer can cancel while the request is still waiting or claimed. Status links stop working after 7 days.</p>\n<h2 id=\"abuse-limits\">Abuse limits</h2>\n<p>The form is unauthenticated, so it is limited in layers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>5 submissions per minute per address (HTTP 429 beyond that);</li>\n<li>at most 3 open requests per address, and 25 per business;</li>\n<li>a hidden honeypot field, plus a minimum time to fill the form. A bot that trips either is told it succeeded, and nothing is stored;</li>\n<li>the address is stored only as a keyed hash, used for the per-address cap.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"for-technicians-the-queue\">For technicians: the queue</h2>\n<p><strong>Support Requests</strong> (needs <code>support:requests</code>) lists the business&#39;s requests. A technician working through an assignment sees the queue for the business they are switched into.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Claim</strong> a request to take it. Claiming is atomic: if two people claim at once, one wins and the other is told who has it.</li>\n<li><strong>Hand off</strong> to someone else who can handle requests in that business. Only people who hold <code>support:requests</code> there, as members or through an assignment, are offered.</li>\n<li><strong>Release</strong> puts it back in the queue. <strong>Complete</strong> or <strong>Cancel</strong> closes it.</li>\n<li><strong>Matched computers</strong>: when the description or RustDesk ID matches computers in the business, they are listed, but only computers <em>you</em> can connect to.</li>\n<li><strong>Connect</strong> is offered to the claimer only, through the normal Connect button for a managed computer or Quick Connect (which records the request on the session).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A request still <code>WAITING</code> after 24 hours expires. Closed requests are anonymized after 30 days, which removes the name, contact details and description.</p>\n<h2 id=\"see-also\">See also</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/docs/technician-guide\">Technician guide</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/docs/access-control\">Access control</a></li>\n</ul>\n",
