    "slug": "offboarding",
    "title": "Access review and offboarding",
    "file": "offboarding.md",
    "html": "<h1 id=\"access-review-and-offboarding\">Access review and offboarding</h1>\n<p><em>Added in v0.20.</em></p>\n<p>When someone leaves, two questions matter: who can still reach a computer, and what did the person who left already have. Rem0te answers both from the same rules that decide every connection, and is explicit about what it <strong>cannot</strong> undo.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-revocation-can-and-cannot-do\">What revocation can and cannot do</h2>\n<p>This is the most important part of the page.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Removing access stops new connections through Rem0te at once.</strong> Memberships, grants and assignments are re-read on every request and every token redemption.</li>\n<li><strong>It does nothing to a RustDesk password the person already saw.</strong> RustDesk accepts the permanent password directly, from any RustDesk client, with no Rem0te involvement. If the person revealed a computer&#39;s password, or connected in a way that released it, that password keeps working until it is changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Rotation is how it is changed.</strong> A new password is staged; the computer applies it on its next check-in (every 3 minutes) and confirms. <strong>Until it confirms, the old password still works</strong>, and Rem0te shows the rotation as <em>pending</em>, never as done. An offline computer stays pending until it comes back.</li>\n<li><strong>Neither ends a RustDesk session that is already open.</strong> End it on the computer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"access-review\">Access review</h2>\n<p>Every computer has an <strong>Access review</strong> panel (needs <code>users:view</code>). It lists everyone who can connect, and how:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Shown as</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Platform Admin</td>\n<td>every computer on the server</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business Owner</td>\n<td>every computer in the business</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Granted access</td>\n<td>a per-computer grant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company-wide</td>\n<td>the computer is company-wide and the person has <code>computers:connect</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Technician (direct / via group)</td>\n<td>an <a href=\"/docs/technicians\">MSP technician assignment</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Each entry says whether it works <strong>right now</strong>. If not, it gives the reason: membership disabled, no connect permission, not started, expired, outside its schedule, or pending approval. Temporary entries show their end.</p>\n<h2 id=\"offboarding-someone\">Offboarding someone</h2>\n<p><strong>Offboarding</strong> (Business Owners in their own business, and Platform Admins). Pick a person; the preview shows, before anything changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>what will be revoked: membership, per-computer grants, technician assignments, group memberships, unredeemed launcher tokens and connection grants;</li>\n<li><strong>the credentials they obtained</strong>: every computer whose password they revealed (refused reveals are excluded), or that released its credential when they connected, with when and how;</li>\n<li><strong>shared passwords</strong>: other computers in the business that use the same password as one they had, including computers they never touched;</li>\n<li>whether the account itself can be suspended.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Running it (needs step-up, <code>permissions.change</code>) does, in order: disable the membership, optionally suspend the account and end every signed-in session, remove grants, revoke assignments, revoke unredeemed tokens, and stage rotation for each computer you ticked. Everything that will be rejected (a suspension not allowed, a computer not on the list) is refused <strong>before</strong> anything changes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"scope\">Scope</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A Business Owner</strong> offboards a person from <strong>their</strong> business only. The account is suspended only if this business is all the person has. Someone who also works elsewhere keeps their account, and only a Platform Admin can suspend it.</li>\n<li><strong>A Platform Admin</strong> (or a holder of <code>technicians:manage</code>, for a technician account) can offboard everywhere: every membership, assignment and group, and every token.</li>\n<li>Access through a <strong>technician group</strong> is not removed by a business-level offboarding. The preview says so; remove the person from the group, or revoke the group&#39;s assignment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"afterwards\">Afterwards</h3>\n<p>The case page shows each step and the <strong>live</strong> status of every rotation: pending (online, waiting for a check-in), pending (offline: the old password works there until it returns), confirmed, or failed. The case is marked complete only when every rotation is confirmed. <strong>Offboarding → Pending rotations</strong> lists every computer in the business whose staged password has not been acknowledged, whoever staged it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"see-also\">See also</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/docs/access-control\">Access control</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/docs/technicians\">Technicians</a></li>\n</ul>\n",
